# Local Netlify folder
.netlify

# Root deps (installed for Netlify Functions, e.g. pdf-lib) — never commit
/node_modules
.claude/*
!.claude/skills/
.claude/skills/**/__pycache__/

# Vite app build artifacts
app/node_modules
app/dist
app-expense/node_modules
app-expense/dist
app-expense/*.tsbuildinfo

# Lockfiles ARE tracked for app/, app-expense/ and app-distributor/ (2026-09-04):
# the Netlify build runs three `npm install`s, and with no lockfile each one
# resolves every ^range against the registry. The 2026-09-04 production deploy
# of #458 timed out at 18 minutes inside those installs while the identical
# deploy preview had built in 14 — a lockfile makes the install a download,
# not a resolution. Regenerate with `npm install --prefix <app>` and commit it.
#
# NOTE (corrected 2026-08-23): Netlify DOES run our build command from
# netlify.toml — it installs and builds app/, app-expense/ and
# app-distributor/ on every deploy. Verified live: production serves bundle
# hashes that exist nowhere in this repo. The older note here claimed the
# opposite and is what justified the (now deleted) build-expense.yml CI job
# that rebuilt and committed public/expense/.
#
# So: SHIP SOURCE ONLY. Do not rebuild-and-commit public/sales-next/,
# public/expense/ or public/distributor/ — it produces asset churn that
# conflicts with every parallel PR (the 2026-08-18 lesson). Those directories
# are still tracked as historical artifacts and are stale by design;
# production never serves them.
.test-build/
